BOOTSTRAPPING THE UNSW TYPESETTER C COMPILER FROM STOCK V6
==========================================================

This directory builds the "typesetter" C compiler (c0, c1, c2)
distributed on the UNSW 3 tape, starting from nothing but a stock
Sixth Edition system.  No binary from the UNSW tape is used.

The stock V6 compiler cannot compile the UNSW sources as they are.
A small number of changes to the V6 driver (cc.c) and to the V6
first pass (c0) are enough.  Those changes are applied by run with
ed, to fresh copies of the pristine V6 sources, so the V6 source
tree itself is never modified.


FILES

	READ_ME		this file
	run		builds everything, see below
	install		installs the result, see below
	itol.s		runtime routine needed by c1, see CHANGES
	c0?.c c0.h	UNSW 3 compiler sources, unchanged
	c1?.c c1.h
	c2?.c c2.h
	cvopt.c table.s

The UNSW sources are taken unchanged from the c_compiler
directory of the UNSW 3 tape.


REQUIREMENTS

1. A stock V6 system with pristine /usr/source/c and
   /usr/source/s1/cc.c (run copies them, it does not edit them).

2. Disk space.  V6 programs (cc, as, ar, cat ...) do NOT report
   write errors; a full file system silently produces truncated
   files and confusing compiler errors.  Check with df first.
   About 600 free blocks are needed on the file system holding
   this directory, and about 300 on the one holding /usr/tcboot.

3. No floating point hardware is required.  The compiler passes
   are linked with -f (software floating point, fptrap), so they
   run on an 11/40 without FPP.


HOW TO USE

	chdir <this directory>
	sh run

run takes several minutes.  Watch the output: apart from the
lines echoed by ed and the step messages, there must be no error
messages.  At the end, run compares stage2 with stage3; nothing
may be printed between the lines

	any output between here and done means failure
	done

If that is the case, install the compiler with

	sh install

install saves the V6 originals in /usr/tcboot/orig and installs

	/bin/cc		the patched V6 driver
	/lib/c0		stage2 c0
	/lib/c1		stage2 c1
	/lib/c2		stage2 c2
	/lib/fc0	same as /lib/c0
	/lib/fc1	same as /lib/c1

To go back to the V6 compiler, copy the files in /usr/tcboot/orig
back to /bin and /lib.


WHAT RUN DOES

step 1	copies the V6 sources of cc.c and c0 into the subdirectory v6
step 2	patches cc.c (see CHANGES)
step 3	makes three variants of the driver which differ only in
	where they look for the compiler passes:

	/usr/tcboot/ncc		c0 = /usr/tcboot/v6/c0,
				c1 and c2 = stock /lib/c1 /lib/c2
	/usr/tcboot/scc		passes in /usr/tcboot/s1 (stage1)
	/usr/tcboot/s2cc	passes in /usr/tcboot/s2 (stage2)

	and the final driver /usr/tcboot/cc, which uses /lib.
	All four are compiled by the stock V6 cc.
step 4	patches c0 (see CHANGES) and builds /usr/tcboot/v6/c0
	with the stock V6 cc
step 5	assembles itol.s into /usr/tcboot/itol.o
step 6	stage1: the UNSW sources compiled by the patched V6
	compiler (ncc), installed in /usr/tcboot/s1
step 7	stage2: the UNSW sources compiled by stage1 (scc),
	installed in /usr/tcboot/s2
step 8	stage3: the UNSW sources compiled by stage2 (s2cc),
	left in this directory
step 9	compares stage3 with stage2; they must be identical,
	then removes stage3

Stage1 is built without -O.  Stage2 and stage3 are built with -O,
as in the original UNSW run script.  Intermediate files are
removed as soon as they are no longer needed.

Stage2 equal to stage3 shows that the bootstrapped compiler
reproduces itself.  A stronger check, still to be done, is to
compile the UNSW sources with -S on a UNSW 3 system using the
compiler from the tape, and compare the assembler output with
that of /usr/tcboot/s2cc -S.  The two must be identical.


CHANGES

cc.c (the V6 driver)

1. The preprocessor tables are too small for c0.h, which has
   about 210 defines.  The symbol table size, written as 200 in
   three places (int symsiz, stab[], and the loop clearing it),
   is now the macro SYMSIZ, 400.  SBSIZE, the space for macro
   bodies, goes from 2000 to 10000.  These are the values used
   by the UNSW cc.c.

2. The V6 driver preprocesses a file only if its first character
   is '#'.  c13.c starts with a comment, so its #include was
   passed straight to c0.  The test is removed: every file is
   preprocessed, as in the UNSW cc.c.  Files without any '#'
   line come out unchanged, apart from comments.

c02.c and c04.c (V6 c0)

3. V6 c0 complains about every structure tag that is not yet
   defined each time a top level structure declaration is seen
   (in blkhed).  c0.h refers to struct hshtab before defining
   it, and c04.c declares a pointer to struct tname, which c0
   never defines.  The check is removed from blkhed, and length()
   now reports an undefined structure when its size is actually
   needed, which is what the UNSW c0 does.

c03.c (V6 c0)

4. The UNSW sources declare formal arguments as register, which
   V6 does not allow.  The register keyword on an argument is
   now simply ignored.  This changes only the speed of stage1,
   never its behaviour; stage2 is produced by the UNSW c0, which
   implements register arguments properly.

c0t.s (V6 c0)

5. fpp is set to 0, exactly as the V6 run script does for /lib/c0.

itol.s

6. c1 calls itol(hi, lo) to build a long from two ints.  It is
   not in the V6 C library, and not among the UNSW sources.  It
   returns the long in r0 (high word) and r1 (low word).

Linking

7. c0 and c1 use floating point to handle floating constants.
   They are linked with -f, so that fptrap simulates the FP11 on
   machines without it.  c2 and cvopt do not need it.


KNOWN LIMITATIONS

- Programs compiled with the new compiler are linked with the V6
  C library.  Code generated for long multiply, divide and
  remainder calls lmul, ldiv, lrem, almul, aldiv and alrem; these
  are not provided here, and the V6 library has a different,
  older ldiv/lrem with three int arguments.  The compiler itself
  does not need them.

- The driver is the V6 one with the changes above.  It does not
  have the features of the UNSW cc (#else, #if expressions,
  nested includes, -D, -I, -E, listings, line markers for c0).
  The UNSW cc.c can be compiled with this compiler, but it calls
  the assembler as "as -u -o", which the V6 as does not accept.

- The V6 driver ignores .s files given on the command line; they
  are passed to ld instead of being assembled.
